-------------------------------------- Going to production -----------------------------------------

- FINAL STEP TO DELIVER OUR APP! WHAT DO WE NEED TO DO TO LAUNCH OUR PRODUCT?

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Going to production" chapter

1️⃣ Now that the application is full-featured and optimized, we are seriously thinking about
    deploying it to production.
    🔥The first step is to package our application in production mode, using ng build.
    This triggers the Ahead of Time compilation, which will compile the HTML templates to
    TypeScript and then to JavaScript. The compilation produces a main.js bundle containing the
    application and the necessary third-party dependencies.

    🔥This AoT compilation can find problems in your templates, that you wouldn’t catch in Just in
    Time mode without navigating to the problematic template. 🔥The AoT compilation is now enabled by
    default since Angular CLI 9.0.0.

    💡For example, modify pony.html to display {{ ponyModel.nam }} in the figcaption, instead of
    {{ ponyModel.name }}. 🔥If you build the application with the AoT compilation disabled
    (with ng build --no-aot), you will see no error in the terminal, despite the fact that the
    name is not displayed if we use the application!

    ⚠️Now run ng serve or ng build, and you should have an error in your terminal looking like:
        ERROR in src/app/pony/pony.html(3,15): : Property 'nam' does not exist on type 'PonyModel'.
        Did you mean 'name'?
    
    💪This is one of the biggest strengths of Angular. If you compile your application with AoT
    compilation enabled (which is the case by default, as the configurations include aot: true by
    default) you can catch many errors in your templates that would otherwise only be found at
    runtime, or even not noticed at all.

    🔥You can go even further. The Angular compiler offers a few options that you can activate in
    your tsconfig.json file, for example extendedDiagnostics to generate additional warnings since
    Angular 13.2.0. If you check the tsconfig.json file, we switched them to errors in Ponyracer.

    There are other options available(https://angular.dev/tools/cli/aot-compiler), but few are
    really useful.

    🔥Another that is worth knowing is preserveWhitespaces, which is now false by default since
    Angular 6. This option removes extra whitespaces. 🔥If you really want a whitespace to be kept,
    you can use a special entity called &ngsp;. It looks like &nbsp; with a typo, but it is not:
    it is a special character that the Angular compiler will transform in a whitespace.
    ⚠️Note that it will only keep one whitespace, even if you add several consecutive ones, like
    &ngsp;&ngsp;. 🔥If you really want to preserve the whitespaces in a template, you can use
    ngPreserveWhitespaces:

        <div ngPreserveWhitespaces>hello     there</div>
    
    🔥But the production configuration also does a bunch of other things. Check out the angular.json
    file, and look for the production configuration. This is where you can tweak what ng build does.

    🔥🔥By default, the CLI targets only modern browsers, but you can configure it by adding a
    .browserslistrc file at the root of the project (ng generate config browserslist). browserslist
    allows defining the targeted browsers thanks to queries like last 2 versions.
    😉Tip: to know which browsers are targeted by your configuration, you can run: npx browserslist.



2️⃣ When you run ng build, the command creates a dist/ponyracer/browser directory in the project,
    containing all the assets resulting from the build phase.

    You can grab all these and deploy them on your favorite HTTP server, and you’re good to go!
    🔥To give it a try quickly, install http-server on your machine using:

        npm i -g http-server
    
    http-server is a simple NodeJS server, ideal to serve a static application on your machine.
    🔥Inside the project directory:

        http-server dist/ponyracer/browser
    
    And then open your browser on http://localhost:8080. Here is our Ponyracer application! You
    can see that everything works without too much trouble.



3️⃣ ⚠️⚠️There is one thing though…​If you go to http://localhost:8080/races, and then reload the page,
    you end up…​ on a 404 error! This is because /races is a route on the client side, but the HTTP
    server doesn’t know that it needs to serve index.html for this request too! When you’ll deploy
    your application to production, you’ll need to think about that as well, and configure your
    HTTP server to properly handle all the routes of your application.

    🔥🔥Another thing you’ll want to enable on your server is the compression of the assets served.
    This can make a huge difference! As I’m writing these lines, running gzip on the main bundle
    makes it shrink to 25% of its original size. This is usually a simple configuration available
    on most HTTP server, so it’s a quick and easy win.

    🔥As we are talking about sizes, a last thing you can do is to add a budget to your assets. By
    default, the CLI adds a budget to the initial load (which regroups all the main JS bundles
    generated that will be loaded initially). You can adjust the warning and error thresholds to
    play with it, or even add new budgets. You can add a budget on all for the whole application
    (https://angular.dev/tools/cli/build#configuring-size-budgets), or for a specific bundle if
    you want. Check out the official documentation for more info. That can be really useful to
    track the size of your application.

    Now you can deploy your application to production. And congratulations! You’re now a ninja with
    Angular 🥷.